            <ArticleTitle>Patterns of pain medication use during last months of life in HIV-infected populations: the experience of an academic outpatient clinic.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>793-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/1049909113503541</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">This article describes the patterns of use of pain medicines of HIV-infected patients during last months of life in an HIV university-affiliated outpatient clinic.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We retrospectively reviewed our databases and identified patients who died over the last 12 months in our clinic. Demographic, clinical, and laboratory information were abstracted.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A total of 41 patients died in our HIV outpatient clinic in a period of 12 months. Opioid analgesics were prescribed for 21 (51%) patients, with 10 (48%) of these patients prescribed short-acting opioid analgesics alone, In all, 11 patients (52%) were on a short-acting and long-acting opioid combination, and 30 (73%) patients experienced pain that was not adequately controlled.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Pain control during the last months of life for this population appears to be suboptimal. Better strategies are needed.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© The Author(s) 2013.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
